`@entry.moth` is the page root. The file's presence makes the folder a page. `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` hold APIs and never become pages. Dependencies never run their entries.

### Entry code

Top-level runtime code in `@entry.moth` becomes a dormant entry. It runs once when that module becomes a page entry. Helper files provide declarations and never own an entry.

The entry has built-in `Error!`. Checked numeric failure and compatible top-level postfix `!` can propagate to the generated terminal handler. Custom errors still need explicit recovery. See @../progress (Progress) for current compiler support.

An empty entry file produces the builder's default page. A metadata-only entry produces a complete document too:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

A nested or aliased insert remains ordinary template composition. Runtime named builder slots are not supported.

`$doc`, `$note` and `$todo` templates are documentation or comment processing. They produce no page output, but the file still creates the page.

### Page fragments

Direct top-level templates in `@entry.moth` become fragments:

- A const fragment (the `#` prefix form) becomes static HTML.
- A runtime fragment (the plain bracket form) runs through the entry.

The builder keeps static and runtime fragments in source order. Assigned or returned templates are ordinary values. Helpers cannot create page fragments.

Runtime fragments publish only after that startup invocation succeeds. Failure leaves static HTML in place and publishes none of the invocation's runtime fragments. Other side effects do not roll back. The generated terminal handler shows a generic safe release notice rather than the application's `Error` message as HTML. Development presentation belongs to the build and dev docs.

### Imported entries stay dormant

Importing a module exposes only its API. It never runs that module's entry or adds its page fragments. An entry-only child has no API to import.
